00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:51,280
And then similarly over here, if there's more x running around,

264
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:54,320
then there's all this x that's bidding for the limited stock

265
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:57,280
of y, and then x becomes cheaper.

266
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:02,240
And of course, we get the same result.

267
00:11:02,240 --> 00:11:03,560
We solve for y instead of x.

268
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:05,000
It's all the same.

269
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,360
Intuitively, we have a barter market.

270
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:10,680
So if you have a barter market, and one thing

271
00:11:10,680 --> 00:11:12,360
is market clear, has market clearing,

272
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:15,160
what does the other thing have to have automatically?

273
00:11:15,160 --> 00:11:16,000
Market clearing.

274
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,040
So if you're trading apples for oranges,

275
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:20,280
and you've got market clearing for apples,

276
00:11:20,280 --> 00:11:22,120
you better have market clearing for oranges,

277
00:11:22,120 --> 00:11:23,720
because otherwise it makes no sense.

278
00:11:23,720 --> 00:11:26,100
Because what does it mean for there to be market clearing?

279
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:30,320
It means that you can buy or sell as many apples

280
00:11:30,320 --> 00:11:31,960
as you want at the market price.

281
00:11:31,960 --> 00:11:34,400
But if the only thing you can buy apples with is oranges,

282
00:11:34,400 --> 00:11:35,940
that would also have to mean that you

283
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:38,400
can buy or sell as many oranges as you want at the market

284
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:38,900
price.

285
00:11:42,080 --> 00:11:45,240
All right, now, just some implications of this.

286
00:11:45,240 --> 00:11:47,880
So if you take a look at this log utility function,

287
00:11:47,880 --> 00:11:50,040
which you've probably seen a bunch of times,

288
00:11:50,040 --> 00:11:53,240
so what would happen to a person if they actually

289
00:11:53,240 --> 00:11:56,000
had 0x, say, or 0y?

290
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:57,720
How bad would that be?

291
00:11:57,720 --> 00:12:00,480
What's the log of 0 again?

292
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:00,980
1?

293
00:12:00,980 --> 00:12:02,120
No.

294
00:12:02,120 --> 00:12:03,560
The log of 1 is 0.

295
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:05,600
What's the log of 0?

296
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:07,840
Infinite.

297
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:09,200
Well, almost.

298
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:11,440
Negative infinite.

299
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:13,800
It's negative infinite to have the log of 0

300
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:15,400
is negative infinity.

301
00:12:15,400 --> 00:12:18,520
So if any person ever were to have 0 of something,

302
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:21,280
that would mean it would be infinitely bad for them.

303
00:12:21,280 --> 00:12:22,760
Be terrible.

304
00:12:22,760 --> 00:12:25,840
All right, so now you're looking, all right, so fine.

305
00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:28,200
So this utility function means everyone's

306
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:30,920
going to want to have at least a little of each good.

307
00:12:30,920 --> 00:12:32,080
That's true.

308
00:12:32,080 --> 00:12:33,080
All right.

309
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:37,400
All right, now, if that's true that it would be infinitely bad

310
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:40,960
to have none of one good, then now

311
00:12:40,960 --> 00:12:44,320
imagine that some people started off with none of one good.

312
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:46,760
Why is it that the market doesn't make them give up

313
00:12:46,760 --> 00:12:48,720
everything they have or almost everything they have just

314
00:12:48,720 --> 00:12:50,300
to get a little bit of x so they don't

315
00:12:50,300 --> 00:12:53,320
have negative infinity utility?

316
00:12:53,320 --> 00:12:53,980
What's going on?

317
00:12:53,980 --> 00:12:55,140
So why does that no way out?

318
00:12:55,140 --> 00:12:59,560
So again, imagine that 90% of people start off with 0x.

319
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:02,400
Are the 10% of people who do have some of both,

320
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:04,840
are those people going to be able to extract almost

321
00:13:04,840 --> 00:13:11,280
everything that 90% have in general equilibrium?

322
00:13:11,280 --> 00:13:13,280
Remember the whole idea of general equilibrium,

323
00:13:13,280 --> 00:13:16,600
the fundamental assumption is that no one individual

324
00:13:16,600 --> 00:13:18,880
can change society.

325
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:19,560
All right.

326
00:13:19,560 --> 00:13:25,280
So that means that if you go and try to offer someone

327
00:13:25,280 --> 00:13:27,040
a really crummy deal, are you going

328
00:13:27,040 --> 00:13:29,840
to be able to get them to go along with your crummy deal?

329
00:13:29,840 --> 00:13:30,680
No.

330
00:13:30,680 --> 00:13:32,520
If you make up a crummy deal, hey,

331
00:13:32,520 --> 00:13:33,800
here's a really terrible deal.

332
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:35,240
Who wants it?

333
00:13:35,240 --> 00:13:37,880
In general equilibrium, will anyone accept your crummy deal?

334
00:13:37,880 --> 00:13:38,380
No.

335
00:13:38,380 --> 00:13:40,120
People say, why should I go and pay

336
00:13:40,120 --> 00:13:44,760
and accept your crummy deal when society offers me a better deal?

337
00:13:44,760 --> 00:13:47,340
And of course, the society in this case is just another word

338
00:13:47,340 --> 00:13:49,240
for competition.

339
00:13:49,240 --> 00:13:52,240
So even though it's true that if someone

340
00:13:52,240 --> 00:13:54,200
were to quarter the market for x,

341
00:13:54,200 --> 00:13:57,160
they would then have a stranglehold over all society.

342
00:13:57,160 --> 00:13:59,040
But if one person is trying to get

343
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:03,000
as any way of getting a quarter of the market for x,

344
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,200
that's not general equilibrium anymore.

345
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:07,320
That is clearly a case of one individual

346
00:14:07,320 --> 00:14:10,440
trying to strategically plan against society.

347
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:13,680
And that's not what general equilibrium is all about.

348
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:16,360
So again, that doesn't mean that that thing couldn't happen,

349
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:18,800
but it can't happen in a general equilibrium model.

350
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:21,240
So general equilibrium models have competition.

351
00:14:21,240 --> 00:14:23,960
And so the fact that someone desperately needs something

352
00:14:23,960 --> 00:14:26,480
doesn't mean they actually are going to pay a lot for it.

353
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:30,360
That all just depends upon supply and demand.

354
00:14:30,360 --> 00:14:31,400
All right, and let's see.

355
00:14:31,400 --> 00:14:34,080
I think you might actually have a homework problem.

356
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:37,520
But it's fun enough that I will mention it right now.

357
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:41,520
All right, so there is a passage from the integral communist

358
00:14:41,520 --> 00:14:44,000
Foucault in the 19th century, but who

359
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:45,920
expressed a view that actually a lot of people

360
00:14:45,920 --> 00:14:47,600
have had where they say, look, here's

361
00:14:47,600 --> 00:14:49,160
the problem being a worker.

362
00:14:49,160 --> 00:14:51,800
Workers have no other way of getting income,

363
00:14:51,800 --> 00:14:54,200
and so they'll settle for anything.

364
00:14:54,200 --> 00:14:58,200
So if someone will work the same amount regardless of the wage,

365
00:14:58,200 --> 00:15:02,120
then what does their labor supply curve look like?

366
00:15:02,120 --> 00:15:04,360
Yes, vertical, exactly.

367
00:15:04,360 --> 00:15:07,480
So we've got a vertical supply curve.

368
00:15:07,480 --> 00:15:10,400
And if you were the only buyer of labor,

369
00:15:10,400 --> 00:15:13,920
and suppose this is the starvation level,

370
00:15:13,920 --> 00:15:15,840
and that's starvation.

371
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:18,160
So if you were the only buyer of labor,

372
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:23,040
then you very well might want to go and offer that amount.

373
00:15:23,040 --> 00:15:24,640
But then Macooning leaves the inference

374
00:15:24,640 --> 00:15:28,360
that this is what workers actually will get.

375
00:15:28,360 --> 00:15:30,640
And again, there's the whole problem here.

376
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:32,240
Well, if there's a demand curve here,

377
00:15:32,240 --> 00:15:34,800
and you try offering them that amount,

378
00:15:34,800 --> 00:15:37,440
then you are below the intersection of supply and demand.

379
00:15:37,440 --> 00:15:39,840
And when you're below that intersection, which of the two

380
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:42,040
things do you get?

381
00:15:42,040 --> 00:15:43,560
It's either a shortage or a surplus.

382
00:15:43,560 --> 00:15:46,240
But I can never remember which one you get.

383
00:15:46,240 --> 00:15:49,600
Which one is it when the price is too low?

384
00:15:49,600 --> 00:15:50,440
Shortage.

385
00:15:50,440 --> 00:15:51,160
Shortage, right.

386
00:15:51,160 --> 00:15:53,440
Well, minimum wage causes surpluses,

387
00:15:53,440 --> 00:15:56,760
as we know from Karger Kruger.

388
00:15:56,760 --> 00:15:58,440
Minimum wage causes surpluses.

389
00:15:58,440 --> 00:16:02,200
So another day, wage that is held above the intersection

390
00:16:02,200 --> 00:16:03,400
causes surpluses.

391
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:05,160
A wage that is below will cause a shortage.

392
00:16:05,160 --> 00:16:07,200
And with competition, when there's a shortage,

393
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:08,040
what do people do?

394
00:16:08,040 --> 00:16:09,800
They bid at the price.

395
00:16:09,800 --> 00:16:12,000
So this is saying that even if Macooning is perfectly

396
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,600
correct about the supply curve, still he

397
00:16:14,600 --> 00:16:15,920
is drawing the wrong inference.

398
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:18,840
As long as there's competition between employers,

399
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:20,760
as long as they are competing with each other,

400
00:16:20,760 --> 00:16:24,320
as long as they are not acting like an executive committee

401
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:26,760
of the bourgeoisie, but instead are independent business

402
00:16:26,760 --> 00:16:29,240
people trying to make money for themselves,

403
00:16:29,240 --> 00:16:32,040
then if the wage is down there, there's

404
00:16:32,040 --> 00:16:33,040
no other way to get it.

405
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:36,080
If the wage is down there, employers

406
00:16:36,080 --> 00:16:38,460
are going to be thinking, well, if I can get more workers,

407
00:16:38,460 --> 00:16:39,380
I can make more money.

408
00:16:39,380 --> 00:16:41,000
But at this wage, I can't get more.

409
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:47,400
So why don't I raise the wage and then steal workers

410
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:49,920
from some other employers?

411
00:16:49,920 --> 00:16:52,640
And again, if you start saying, well, if you do that,

412
00:16:52,640 --> 00:16:53,800
then they'll get mad at you.

413
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:54,680
They'll retaliate.

414
00:16:54,680 --> 00:16:56,440
Again, is that general equilibrium thinking

415
00:16:56,440 --> 00:16:57,980
that I'll go and steal some workers,

416
00:16:57,980 --> 00:17:00,200
and then the other employers will retaliate?

417
00:17:00,200 --> 00:17:02,280
That's not general equilibrium thinking.

418
00:17:02,280 --> 00:17:04,400
Again, it's conceivable that's true.

419
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:08,000
But if you have thought that, it is not general equilibrium.

420
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:09,600
But it is only general equilibrium

421
00:17:09,600 --> 00:17:11,320
if that has not been thought.

422
00:17:11,320 --> 00:17:13,980
If this has not been considered, if this is just off the table,

423
00:17:13,980 --> 00:17:17,160
that kind of thing would happen.

424
00:17:17,160 --> 00:17:18,800
OK, so questions.

425
00:17:18,800 --> 00:17:21,120
Questions here.

426
00:17:21,120 --> 00:17:24,640
So these are two simple examples of general equilibrium.

427
00:17:24,640 --> 00:17:28,480
And now we're going to go and generalize it further.

428
00:17:28,480 --> 00:17:31,960
Now, I could go and really torture you with this for weeks.

429
00:17:31,960 --> 00:17:33,080
But I don't want to.

430
00:17:33,080 --> 00:17:34,880
And I don't know anyone who's ever,

431
00:17:34,880 --> 00:17:36,720
well, I guess people who do it for a living,

432
00:17:36,720 --> 00:17:38,520
I guess they've profited from me torturing with it for weeks.

433
00:17:38,520 --> 00:17:40,720
Because then they go and extend the models and so on.

434
00:17:40,720 --> 00:17:42,400
But out of everyone else, I've never

435
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:45,000
known anyone to actually get any kind of intellectual profit

436
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,840
out of drawing it out.

437
00:17:47,840 --> 00:17:51,720
So instead, I'm just going to hit the high points

438
00:17:51,720 --> 00:17:56,200
and try to help you understand exactly how these models work.

439
00:17:56,200 --> 00:17:58,660
So that when economists around you are talking about them,

440
00:17:58,660 --> 00:18:01,080
you can say, oh, I understand what's going on here,

441
00:18:01,080 --> 00:18:02,460
even though I didn't bother going

442
00:18:02,460 --> 00:18:05,120
into all the details, which, of course, almost everybody

443
00:18:05,120 --> 00:18:06,400
forgets anyway.

444
00:18:06,400 --> 00:18:08,840
So better to go and teach you a small amount

445
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:10,300
and then expect you to remember it,

446
00:18:10,300 --> 00:18:14,120
rather than a large amount that can be quickly forgotten.

447
00:18:14,120 --> 00:18:17,680
This is a very big result in educational psychology,

448
00:18:17,680 --> 00:18:20,000
is just how much material people forget.

449
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:23,840
So in this class, I do try to teach you some math.

450
00:18:23,840 --> 00:18:26,440
But my general policy is I only teach you

451
00:18:26,440 --> 00:18:29,240
a math I would actually test you on, conceivably.

452
00:18:29,240 --> 00:18:31,640
So I won't deal with anything in addition to that.

453
00:18:31,640 --> 00:18:34,480
So I'll teach you the stuff that I think is worthwhile actually

454
00:18:34,480 --> 00:18:34,840
learning.

455
00:18:34,840 --> 00:18:37,080
And then the other stuff, if you're curious about it,

456
00:18:37,080 --> 00:18:40,520
you can, of course, do more.

457
00:18:40,520 --> 00:18:44,240
So now I just want to give you a taste of a very general general

458
00:18:44,240 --> 00:18:45,800
equilibrium model.

459
00:18:45,800 --> 00:18:49,440
So let's now go and assume that we've got i consumers.

460
00:18:49,440 --> 00:18:50,440
We already did that.

461
00:18:50,440 --> 00:18:52,080
And now let's say we got k commodities.

462
00:18:52,080 --> 00:18:53,960
So no longer do we just have two commodities.

463
00:18:53,960 --> 00:18:57,160
Now we can have any number of commodities.

464
00:18:57,160 --> 00:18:59,040
And now we start adding on some assumptions

465
00:18:59,040 --> 00:19:00,960
that sound a bit weird.

466
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:03,000
In general, any time there's an economic model,

467
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,400
a mathematical model with some weird assumptions,

468
00:19:05,400 --> 00:19:08,440
there's a reason for the weird assumption.

469
00:19:08,440 --> 00:19:10,400
If you don't have that assumption,

470
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:12,160
you either can't solve the model,

471
00:19:12,160 --> 00:19:15,960
or the results of the model seem crazy to the theorists.

472
00:19:15,960 --> 00:19:18,080
All right, so here's their assumption.

473
00:19:18,080 --> 00:19:21,120
Commodity consumption must be non-negative.

474
00:19:21,120 --> 00:19:24,080
Well, of course, commodity consumption is non-negative.

475
00:19:24,080 --> 00:19:25,560
Of course.

476
00:19:25,560 --> 00:19:28,160
Why are people bothering to make a big deal out

477
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:30,360
of the consumption must be non-negative?

478
00:19:30,360 --> 00:19:32,520
Because there are some theorems that they're

479
00:19:32,520 --> 00:19:34,840
going to invoke that require non-negativity.

480
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:37,200
So they want to explicitly state that that commodity

481
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:38,600
consumption is non-negative.

482
00:19:38,600 --> 00:19:41,400
So then no one can mathematically complain.

483
00:19:41,400 --> 00:19:43,840
They're not entitled to use the theorem.

484
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:46,720
All right, now for the assumption,

485
00:19:46,720 --> 00:19:48,760
we'll assume that utility is strictly increasing

486
00:19:48,760 --> 00:19:50,440
in all commodities.

487
00:19:50,440 --> 00:19:53,240
So again, this u sub i of x, this

488
00:19:53,240 --> 00:19:56,480
is saying that every individual has strictly increased

489
00:19:56,480 --> 00:19:58,720
utility in every commodity.

490
00:19:58,720 --> 00:20:26,720
So that x is a vector.


